    42	## [Experiment-Material/](Experiment-Material/)
    43	<a id="material"></a>
    44	Contains the material used for the experiment, and, specifically, the following subdirectories:
    45	
    46	### [Google-Forms/](Experiment-Material/Google-Forms/)
    47	Contains (as PDF documents) the questionnaires submitted to the ten experimental groups.
    48	
    49	### [Task-Sources/](Experiment-Material/Task-Sources/)
    50	Contains, for each experimental group (G-1...G-10), the sources used to produce the Google Forms, and, specifically:
    51	- The cover letter (Letter.docx).
    52	- A directory for each experimental task (Lambda 1, Lambda 2, Comp 1, Comp 2, MRF 1, MRF 2, Lambda Comparison, Comp Comparison, MRF Comparison). Each directory contains: (i) the exercise text (in both Word and .txt format), the source code snippet, and its .png image to be used in the form. **Note:** the "Comparison" tasks do not have any exercise as the purpose is always the same, i.e., to compare the (perceived) understandability of the snippets and return the results of the comparison. 
    53	
    54	### [Code-Examples-Table1/](Experiment-Material/Code-Examples-Table1/)
    55	Contains the source code snippets used as objects of the study (the same you can find under "Task-Sources/"), named as reported in Table 1.
    56	
    57	
    58	
    59	
    60	
    61	
    62	
    63	
    64	## Results' directory and files
    65	<a id="results"></a>
    66	
    67	### [raw-responses/](raw-responses/)
    68	Contains, as spreadsheets, the raw responses provided by the study participants through Google forms.
    69	
    70	### [raw-results-RQ1/](raw-results-RQ1/)
    71	Contains the raw results for RQ1. Specifically, the directory contains a subdirectory for each group (G1-G10). Each subdirectory contains:
    72	- For each user (named using their Prolific  IDs, a directory containing, for each question (Q1-Q6) the produced python code (Qn.py) its output (QnR.txt) and its StdErr output (QnErr.txt).
    73	- "expected-outputs/": A directory containing the expected outputs for each task (Qn.txt).
    74	
    75	#### [working-results/RQ1-RQ2-files-for-statistical-analysis/](working-results/RQ1-RQ2-files-for-statistical-analysis/)
    76	Contains three .csv files used as input for conducting the statistical analysis and drawing the graphs for addressing the first two research questions of the study. Specifically:
    77	
    78	- [ConstructUsage.csv](working-results/RQ1-RQ2-files-for-statistical-analysis/ConstructUsage.csv) contains the declared frequency usage of the three functional constructs object of the study. This file is used to draw Figure 4. The file contains an entry for each participant, reporting the (text-coded) frequency of construct usage for Comprehension, Lambda, and MRF.
    79	
    80	- [RQ1.csv](working-results/RQ1-RQ2-files-for-statistical-analysis/RQ1.csv) contains the collected data used for the mixed-effect logistic regression relating the use of functional constructs with the correctness of the change task, as well as the logistic regression relating the use of map/reduce/filter functions with the correctness of the change task. 
    81	    The csv file contains an entry for each answer provided by each subject, and features the following columns:
    82	    - *Group*: experimental group to which the participant is assigned
    83	    - *User*: user ID
    84	    - *Time*: task time in seconds
    85	    - *Approvals*: number of approvals on previous tasks performed on Prolific
    86	    - *Student*: whether the participant declared themselves as a student
    87	    - *Section*: section of the questionnaire (lambda, comp, or mrf)
    88	    - *Construct*: specific construct being presented (same as "Section" for lambda and comp, for mrf it says whether it is a map, reduce, or filter)
    89	    - *Question*: question id, from Q1 to Q6, indicate the ordering of the question
    90	    - *MainFactor*: main factor treatment for the given question - "f" for functional, "p" for procedural counterpart
    91	    - *Outcome*: TRUE if the task was correctly performed, FALSE otherwise
    92	    - *Complexity*: cyclomatic complexity of the construct (empty for mrf)
    93	    - *UsageFrequency*: usage frequency of the given construct
    94	
    95	
    96	- [RQ1Paired-RQ2.csv](working-results/RQ1-RQ2-files-for-statistical-analysis/RQ1Paired-RQ2.csv) contains the collected data used for the ordinal logistic regression of the relationship between the perceived ease of understanding of the functional constructs and (i) participants' usage frequency, and (ii) constructs' complexity (except for map/reduce/filter).
    97	    The file features a row for each participant, and the columns are the following:
    98	    
    99	    - *Group*: experimental group to which the participant is assigned
   100	    - *User*: user ID
   101	    - *Time*: task time in seconds
   102	    - *Approvals*: number of approvals on previous tasks performed on Prolific
   103	    - *Student*: whether the participant declared themselves as a student
   104	    - *LambdaF*: result for the change task related to a lambda construct
   105	    - *LambdaP*: result for the change task related to the procedural counterpart of a lambda construct
   106	    - *CompF*: result for the change task related to a comprehension construct
   107	    - *CompP*: result for the change task related to the procedural counterpart of a comprehension construct
   108	    - *MrfF*: result for the change task related to an MRF construct
   109	    - *MrfP*: result for the change task related to the procedural counterpart of a MRF construct
   110	    - *LambdaComp*: perceived understandability level for the comparison task (RQ2) between a lambda and its procedural counterpart
   111	    - *CompComp*: perceived understandability level for the comparison task (RQ2) between a comprehension and its procedural counterpart
   112	    - *MrfComp*: perceived understandability level for the comparison task (RQ2) between a MRF and its procedural counterpart
   113	    - *LambdaCompCplx*: cyclomatic complexity of the lambda construct involved in the comparison task (RQ2)
   114	    - *CompCompCplx*: cyclomatic complexity of the comprehension construct involved in the comparison task (RQ2)
   115	    - *MrfCompType*: type of MRF construct (map, reduce, or filter) used in the comparison task (RQ2)
   116	    - *LambdaUsageFrequency*: self-declared usage frequency on lambda constructs
   117	    - *CompUsageFrequency*: self-declared usage frequency on comprehension constructs
   118	    - *MrfUsageFrequency*: self-declared usage frequency on MRF constructs
   119	    - *LambdaComparisonAssessment*: outcome of the manual assessment of the answer to the "check question" required for the lambda comparison ("yes" means valid, "no" means wrong, "moderate_chatgpt" and "extreme_chatgpt" are the results of GPTZero)
   120	    - *CompComparisonAssessment*: as above, but for comprehension
   121	    - *MrfComparisonAssessment*: as above, but for MRF
   122	    
   123	
   124	### [working-results/inter-rater-RQ3-files/](working-results/inter-rater-RQ3-files/)
   125	This directory contains four .csv files used as input for computing the inter-rater agreement for the manual labeling used for addressing RQ3. Specifically, you will find one file for each functional construct, i.e., comprehension.csv, lambda.csv, and mrf.csv, and a different file used for highlighting the reasons why participants prefer to use the procedural paradigm, i.e., procedural.csv.
   126	
   127	
   128	### [working-results/RQ2ManualValidation.csv](working-results/RQ2ManualValidation.csv)
   129	This file contains the results of the manual validation being done to sanitize the answers provided by our participants used for addressing RQ2. Specifically, we coded the behaviour description using four different levels: (i) correct ("yes"), (ii) somewhat correct ("partial"), (iii) wrong ("no"), and (iv) automatically generated. The file features a row for each participant, and the columns are the following:
   130	
   131	- *ID*: ID we used to refer the participant in the paper's qualitative analysis
   132	- *Group*: experimental group to which the participant is assigned
   133	- *ProlificID*: user ID
   134	- *Comparison for lambda construct description*: answer provided by the user for the lambda comparison task
   135	- *Final Classification*: our assessment of the lambda comparison answer
   136	- *Comparison for comprehension description*: answer provided by the user for the comprehension comparison task
   137	- *Final Classification*: our assessment of the comprehension comparison answer
   138	- *Comparison for MRF description*: answer provided by the user for the MRF comparison task
   139	- *Final Classification*: our assessment of the MRF comparison answer
   140	
   141	
   142	### [working-results/RQ3ManualValidation.xlsx](working-results/RQ3ManualValidation.xlsx)
   143	This file contains the results of the open coding applied to address our third research question. Specifically, you will find four sheets, one for each functional construct and one for the procedural paradigm. Each sheet reports the provided answers together with the categories assigned to them. 
   144	Each sheet contains the following columns:
   145	
   146	- *ID*: ID we used to refer the participant in the paper's qualitative analysis
   147	- *Group*: experimental group to which the participant is assigned
   148	- *ProlificID*: user ID (as in the tables from the quantitative analysis)    
   149	- *<Question being asked>*:  question asked to the user
   150	- *Final Classification*: The outcome of our categorization according to the taxonomy shown in Table 10.
   151	
   152	
   153	## Scripts to reproduce the results and instructions for running them
   154	<a id="scripts"></a>
   155	
   156	### [FuncConstructs-Statistics.r](FuncConstructs-Statistics.r) 
   157	This file contains an R script that you can reuse to re-run all the analyses conducted and discussed in the paper. 
   158	
   159	### [FuncConstructs-Statistics.ipynb](FuncConstructs-Statistics.ipynb)
   160	This file contains the code to re-execute all the analysis conducted in the paper as a Jupyter Notebook (using the R Kernel).
   161	
   162	### [run-analysis.sh](run-analysis.sh)
   163	This script can be used to run the R script `FuncConstructs-Statistics.r` using a Docker container (see Option 1 below) in Unix operating systems.
   164	
   165	### [run-analysis.bat](run-analysis.bat)
   166	This script can be used to run the R script `FuncConstructs-Statistics.r` using a Docker container (see Option 1 below) in Windows operating systems (power shell recommended).
   167	
   168	### [run-jupyter-container.sh](run-jupyter-container.sh)
   169	This script can be used to run a local Jupyter server (with R kernel and all required packages) from a Docker container (see Option 3 below) in Unix operating system.
   170	
   171	### [run-jupyter-container.bat](run-jupyter-container.bat)
   172	This script can be used to run a local Jupyter server (with R kernel and all required packages) from a Docker container (see Option 3 below) in Windows operating systems (power shell recommended).
   173	
   174	
   175	### How to Run the scripts
   176	There are four options to run the scripts. In all cases, one has first to open a shell terminal window (e.g., bash or sh in Unixes) in the replication package directory. For Windows, we suggest to use a Power Shell.
   177	
   178	1. **Running the R script using Dockerized R installation**: this is the simplest option, and it simply requires a running Docker engine. In **Unix (MacOS, Linux)**, to produce the results, one has to run the shell script "run-analysis.sh" (e.g., by typing `sh run-analysis.sh` or simply `./run-analysis.sh` after making it executable).  In **Windows**, one has to run the script "run-analysis.bat" instead (by typing `.\run-analysis.bat`). This script (either .sh or .bat) will:
   179	    - Pull a docker image named `mdipenta/rexp` which contains an R installation with all required packages.
   180	    - Run R from the container created from the image and produce the paper's results under a directory named `results/`.
   181	    
   182	    - **Note:** An alternative would be to run everything from inside the container, after running it in interactive mode. To this aim, please execute the following commands:
   183	          1. In **Unix**: `docker run -v${PWD}:/data --rm -ti --name shell mdipenta/rexp:latest bash` in **Windows**: `docker run -v %cd%:/data --rm -ti --name shell mdipenta/rexp:latest bash`    
   184	          2. `cd data`
   185	          3. `R --no-save < FuncConstructs-Statistics.r`
   186	    After exiting the container, the "results" directory will be again populated with the study results.
   187	        
   188	2. **Running the R script from own R installation**: this option works if one has an R installation already (or wants to use an R installation) without relying on the Docker image. The steps to be followed are:
   189	   - Uncomment the `install.packages(..)` instruction in the first lines of the script. This will allow for the installation of the required packages.
   190	   - Just run, from the current directory, the script `FuncConstructs-Statistics.r`, using the command `Rscript FuncConstructs-Statistics.r` (making sure the directory containing Rscript is in your PATH, this should work fine in Unixes, it might require to modify the PATH environment variable in Windows). Should you experience problems with the first part of the script (installations), try to execute the `install.packages(..)` statement from your R GUI, and then run the script again.
   191	   
   192	3. **Using the Jupyter Notebook using a Dockerized Jupyter lab with R kernel**: this option allows for opening the Jupyter Notebook with all results without having to install Jupyter with the R kernel, nor all the required R packages. The steps required are:
   193	    - Run the `./run-jupyter-container.sh` (**Unix**) or `.\run-jupyter-container.bat` (**Windows**). It will download the `mdipenta/myjupyter` image and run Jupyter lab from it.
   194	    - Open a browser on [localhost:8888](http://localhost:8888) (or if it does not work, [127.0.0.1:8888)](http://127.0.0.1:8888) and, when being asked for a password, type `docker`. 
